 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Adaptive Octree Marching Tetrahedra](3897-adaptive-octree-marching-tetrahedra.md)
-An isosurface extraction method that uses an adaptive octree to subdivide a 3D volume only where the scalar field contains the target isovalue, applying the Marching Tetrahedra algorithm at leaf nodes to generate the mesh. It differs from its parent [Marching Tetrahedrons](3720-marching-tetrahedrons.md) by introducing spatial adaptivity via octree subdivision, reducing computation in empty regions, and from [Naive Isosurface Extraction](4328-naive-isosurface-extraction.md) by avoiding uniform grid traversal. Choose it when processing sparse or complex datasets where uniform grid traversal is inefficient.
+Adaptive Octree Marching Tetrahedra uses an adaptive octree to subdivide a 3D volume only where the scalar field contains the target isovalue. At leaf nodes (1x1x1 cubes), the volume is decomposed into tetrahedra, and the Marching Tetrahedra algorithm is applied to generate the mesh. It solves the problem of inefficient uniform-grid processing by reducing memory usage and computational cost for sparse or complex datasets. Unlike Naive Isosurface Extraction which processes every voxel, this method explicitly constructs an octree data structure to manage resolution adaptivity before decomposing leaf nodes into tetrahedra for precise surface intersection.
 
 ### [Naive Isosurface Extraction](4328-naive-isosurface-extraction.md)
-Naive Isosurface Extraction is a surface extraction algorithm that processes each tetrahedron independently to generate a collection of individual, unconnected polygons. It differs from standard Marching Tetrahedra by iterating through all pairs of sample points to find intersections with an isosurface, rather than using the standard 14-case lookup table based on vertex signs. Choose it when simple, unconnected polygon generation is sufficient and topological connectivity is not required.
+Naive Isosurface Extraction is a surface extraction algorithm that processes each tetrahedron independently to generate a collection of individual, unconnected polygons. It differs from standard Marching Tetrahedra by iterating through all tetrahedra and their edges to find intersections with an isosurface, rather than using the standard 14-case lookup table based on vertex signs.
 
 ### [GPU Vertex Shader Isosurface Extraction](6706-gpu-vertex-shader-isosurface-extraction.md)
-GPU Vertex Shader Isosurface Extraction is a surface extraction technique that computes isosurfaces on programmable graphics hardware by drawing a single quadrilateral for each tetrahedron and computing intersection locations within the vertex shader. It sets itself apart from CPU-based or fragment-shader-based methods by leveraging the vertex shader's programmability to reposition vertices to exact intersection points, effectively moving the geometric computation from the CPU to the GPU's vertex processing stage. Choose it when high-performance parallel surface generation is required.
+GPU Vertex Shader Isosurface Extraction is a surface extraction method that computes isosurfaces by mapping the topology of marching tetrahedra directly onto the vertex shader stage of the graphics pipeline. It solves the problem of efficient parallel surface generation by drawing a single quadrilateral per tetrahedron and repositioning its vertices to the exact isosurface intersection points within the shader. It differs from its parent Marching Tetrahedrons by offloading the topological case analysis and geometric interpolation to the GPU vertex shader, rather than computing them on the CPU or using geometry shaders.
+
+### [Barycentric Mapping for Material Interfaces](9849-barycentric-mapping-for-material-interfaces.md)
+Barycentric Mapping for Material Interfaces is an algorithm for reconstructing piecewise two-manifold boundary surfaces between multiple materials in a grid where each cell contains fractional volumetric information for each material. It transforms the reconstruction problem to analyze a dual data set where vertices have associated barycentric coordinate tuples representing material fractions. It sets itself apart from standard isosurface extraction by operating directly on volume-fraction data in unstructured triangular meshes, using the geometric properties of Voronoi cells in barycentric space to define interfaces rather than scalar field thresholds. Unlike SLIC, Youngs, or Gueffier algorithms which produce generally discontinuous interfaces and are limited to 2D or specific normal estimates, this algorithm produces a crack-free piecewise two-manifold, handles any number of materials per element, works on any grid structure (via tetrahedral conversion), and does not rely on application-specific hydrodynamics knowledge.
+
+### [3-phase Marching Tetrahedra](11053-3-phase-marching-tetrahedra.md)
+An algorithm to extract isosurfaces from tetrahedral meshes on GPU that avoids sparse output arrays by using a prefix sum to determine output offsets, allowing separate handling of cells generating 1 vs 2 triangles. It builds on Marching Tetrahedrons by optimizing the memory layout and execution divergence of the triangle generation phase.
+
+### Tetrahedral Volume Integration
+A method to compute the volume enclosed by an iso-surface generated from tetrahedral decomposition by summing the volumes of tetrahedra parts that lie inside the iso-surface [S8, unofficial]. For each tetrahedron, if it is entirely outside, it is ignored; if entirely inside, its full volume is added; if intersected, the volume of the intersected part is computed by forming small tetrahedra with the generated triangles and adding their volumes [S8, unofficial]. Cell edge length is the unit of measurement (e.g., radius, edge length) relative to the grid, with a value of 1 [S8, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of tetrahedra (6 times the number of voxels) [G]. Each tetrahedron is processed independently in constant time [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$, where $M$ is the number of output triangles [G]. The lookup table is constant size [G].
-*   **Performance:** The algorithm generates approximately 4x more geometry than [Marching Cubes](link-to-marching-cubes), making it slower in both computation and rendering [S4, unofficial][S5]. Benchmarks show MT producing significantly more vertices and faces than MC for the same input (e.g., 58,580 verts vs 15,268 verts in one demo) [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Produces a manifold surface without topological ambiguities (e.g., the "tetrahedron ambiguity" resolved by the decomposition) [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It is simpler to implement than Marching Cubes due to fewer cases (16 total, reducible to 3 symmetric cases) [S4, unofficial][S5]. It works on unstructured grids as well as structured grids [S5].
-*   **Limitations:** Higher vertex count leads to increased memory usage and rendering cost [S4, unofficial]. The tetrahedral decomposition can introduce slight geometric bias depending on how the cube is split [S4, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When a manifold surface is required and topological correctness is more important than performance or mesh size [S4, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When performance is critical and the ambiguity of Marching Cubes can be handled or is acceptable [S4, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels. Each voxel is decomposed into 6 tetrahedra, and each tetrahedron is processed in constant time [S7, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the output mesh. The mesh size is approximately 4x larger than Marching Cubes due to higher vertex count [S5, unofficial][S6].
+- **Performance:** Slower than Marching Cubes due to increased geometry and computational overhead [S2, S5, unofficial]. Well-suited for GPU parallelization as each tetrahedron is independent [S7, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Guarantees manifold surfaces without ambiguity; works on unstructured tetrahedral grids; simpler case analysis (8 cases, reducible to 3 by symmetry) [S5, unofficial][S6][S7, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Higher memory usage and rendering cost due to more triangles; decomposition consistency must be maintained across voxel boundaries [S5, unofficial][S6].
+- **When to Use:** When topological correctness is critical and ambiguity resolution is required; when working with unstructured tetrahedral meshes; when GPU acceleration is available [S6][S7, unofficial].
+- **When Not to Use:** When performance is paramount and approximate geometry is acceptable; when memory is constrained [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Marching Tetrahedrons was introduced by Doi and Koide [S4, unofficial]. It was developed as an alternative to [Marching Cubes](link-to-marching-cubes) to address the topological ambiguities present in the cubic algorithm [S4, unofficial][S5]. A historical advantage was that it avoided the patent that covered Marching Cubes [S4, unofficial][S5]. The algorithm is essentially a triangle-driven approach, which has led to significant research by GPGPU groups on accelerating it in hardware [S5]. It is often implemented as a direct port of Paul Bourke's C implementation [S4, unofficial].
+Marching Tetrahedrons was introduced by Doi and Koide as an alternative to Marching Cubes to resolve topological ambiguities [S5, unofficial]. It decomposes cubic voxels into tetrahedra, leveraging the fact that a tetrahedron has only 4 vertices, leading to fewer cases (8 instead of 256) and no ambiguous configurations [S5, unofficial][S6]. The algorithm has been implemented in various contexts, including WebGL demos and scientific visualization tools [S5, unofficial][S6]. It is used in medical imaging, industrial machinery testing, and scientific visualization [S3, unofficial].
 
